WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.163
You're on a train, sending a message to a friend, just as it goes into a tunnel.

2
00:00:08.163 --> 00:00:10.348
The message turns red: not sent.

3
00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:11.509
So you tap Retry.

4
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:15.977
Out of the tunnel, your friend writes back: why did you send that twice?

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.140
The red mark looked like a fact about the message.

6
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.709
It was only a fact about your phone: it had stopped waiting.

7
00:00:22.709 --> 00:00:24.315
The first copy got through.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:31.013
Kafka, a program that keeps logs of messages and lets other programs read them, sets the same trap.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:35.226
The program that writes is the producer, inside the payments app.

10
00:00:35.226 --> 00:00:44.623
It sends each payment to the leader, the server that adds it to the partition's log, and waits for an answer: the offset, the payment's place in the log.

11
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:45.728
Watch pay-16.

12
00:00:45.728 --> 00:00:50.083
It reaches the leader, and the leader writes it at offset 16.

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:51.796
Then the answer is lost.

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.322
After a while, all the producer knows is that it heard nothing.

15
00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.105
So it retries, on its own.

16
00:00:58.105 --> 00:01:04.073
The leader can't tell a retry from a new record, so pay-16 goes in again, at offset 17.

17
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:07.733
Every reader of this log now sees the payment twice.

18
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.483
One coffee, charged twice.

19
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:12.713
Customers have a gift for noticing exactly that.

20
00:01:12.713 --> 00:01:17.828
So the point really is: a retry is only safe if the leader can recognise it.

21
00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:19.713
There's a second problem.

22
00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:23.543
The producer keeps up to five requests in flight at once.

23
00:01:23.543 --> 00:01:27.440
Say pay-17's request times out, while pay-18 gets through.

24
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.704
The retry of pay-17 lands after pay-18.

25
00:01:30.704 --> 00:01:37.149
If pay-18 were a refund, it would now overtake its payment, inside one partition.

26
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:39.549
You could send one request at a time.

27
00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:47.395
That keeps the order, but each connection then waits a round trip for every batch, and the lost answer still gives you a duplicate.

28
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.705
What we'd love is for the leader to say: oh, it's you again, I already have that one.

29
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.246
Back to the train.

30
00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:58.703
Suppose every message carries your name and a number: Sam, message 16.

31
00:01:58.703 --> 00:02:05.579
And your friend keeps a little note for each sender: the next number she expects, and the last five she got.

32
00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:06.703
Resent 16?

33
00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:08.483
She already has it.

34
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:12.136
No second copy; she just replies again.

35
00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:13.354
18 before 17?

36
00:02:13.354 --> 00:02:16.445
She says: wait, send me 17 first.

37
00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:17.493
Let's map it.

38
00:02:17.493 --> 00:02:19.411
Your name is the producer id.

39
00:02:19.411 --> 00:02:21.659
The number is the sequence number.

40
00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:27.412
Your friend is the leader, and her note is what the leader remembers for each producer.

41
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.237
Here's the real thing.

42
00:02:29.237 --> 00:02:33.998
At start-up, the producer gets a producer id from the broker: 5.

43
00:02:33.998 --> 00:02:39.652
It numbers its records for each partition, so pay-16 has sequence number 16.

44
00:02:39.867 --> 00:02:44.833
The leader writes it at offset 16, and notes: producer 5, next 17.

45
00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:47.091
Then the answer is lost again.

46
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:50.690
The retry carries the same id and the same number.

47
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:59.248
The leader finds 16 among the last five it remembers, writes nothing, and answers with the offset it gave the first time: 16.

48
00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:01.695
This is the idempotent producer.

49
00:03:01.695 --> 00:03:07.066
Idempotent means doing something twice has the same effect as doing it once.

50
00:03:07.066 --> 00:03:10.035
It has been on by default since Kafka 3.0.

51
00:03:10.233 --> 00:03:13.706
Now the reordering case, with idempotence on.

52
00:03:13.706 --> 00:03:17.024
pay-17 times out, and pay-18 arrives first.

53
00:03:17.024 --> 00:03:18.721
The leader expects 17.

54
00:03:18.721 --> 00:03:20.573
What does it do with 18?

55
00:03:20.573 --> 00:03:21.808
Pause and guess.

56
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.395
It refuses it, with an error called out of order sequence number.

57
00:03:29.395 --> 00:03:34.805
Then the producer resends 17, then 18, and both are written once, in send order.

58
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.418
It needs three settings: acks=all, retries above zero, and at most five requests in flight.

59
00:03:41.418 --> 00:03:45.509
Five is exactly how many batches the leader can recognise.

60
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.122
And here's the trap.

61
00:03:47.122 --> 00:03:53.663
Set acks=1 for speed, without mentioning idempotence, and the producer quietly turns it off.

62
00:03:53.663 --> 00:03:54.445
No warning.

63
00:03:54.445 --> 00:03:57.929
Polite of it, and terrible manners for a payment.

64
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.872
So set enable.idempotence to true by name.

65
00:04:00.872 --> 00:04:05.763
Then the same conflict stops the producer at start-up, where you'll see it.

66
00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:07.443
And know where it stops.

67
00:04:07.443 --> 00:04:09.781
It covers this producer's own retries.

68
00:04:09.781 --> 00:04:16.119
If your code catches a TimeoutException and calls send() again, that's a new record, with a new number.

69
00:04:16.119 --> 00:04:18.580
That's you, tapping Retry in the tunnel.

70
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:22.162
A restarted program gets a new producer id, too.

71
00:04:22.162 --> 00:04:28.529
Copies across those lines need the payment's own idempotency key, checked by the consumer.

72
00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:32.721
In Java and Kotlin, it's two lines in the producer's settings.

73
00:04:32.721 --> 00:04:35.936
Turn idempotence on by name, and keep acks at all.

74
00:04:36.733 --> 00:04:39.115
So why did your friend get two copies?

75
00:04:39.115 --> 00:04:42.500
Your phone retried a message that had already arrived.

76
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.951
With a name and a number on every message, the receiver spots the copy.

77
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:51.237
Retries are safe only when the receiver can recognise them.

78
00:04:51.237 --> 00:04:53.184
Turn idempotence on by name.

79
00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:54.938
One quick check.

80
00:04:54.938 --> 00:04:59.842
Your code catches a TimeoutException for pay-20 and sends it again.

81
00:04:59.842 --> 00:05:01.160
Idempotence is on.

82
00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.722
Can pay-20 end up in the log twice?

83
00:05:03.722 --> 00:05:04.893
Pause and think.

84
00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:08.356
Yes.

85
00:05:08.356 --> 00:05:14.216
Your second send gets a new sequence number, 21, so the leader sees a new record.

86
00:05:14.216 --> 00:05:18.412
Only the payment's own idempotency key can catch that one.
